    <innerclass refid="classClinics" prot="public">Clinics</innerclass>
    <briefdescription>
<para><ref refid="classService" kindref="compound">Service</ref> entity bound to the <computeroutput>clinics</computeroutput> table; the organisation root for clinic cases. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>This header declares the <ref refid="classClinics" kindref="compound">Clinics</ref> class, a very small C++ type that stands for one clinic in the <computeroutput>clinics</computeroutput> database table. Like the other service providers, it holds almost no logic of its own: it only names the table and lets its parent class <ref refid="classService" kindref="compound">Service</ref> build the SQL. You create one by passing in a data wrapper made from a client request, and the parent stores that wrapper. This table is also special: it is the organisation that every clinic case (patient, visit, report, ...) ultimately belongs to.</para>
<para>WHY THIS FILE MATTERS IN THE SERVER: <ref refid="classClinics" kindref="compound">Clinics</ref> is both a service provider (clinics can be created, read, updated and deleted through the service/clinic routes) and the organisation root used by the permission system. <ref refid="classCase_1a7845d0ed5ca20c2a1de1adeadae345ae" kindref="member">Case::ORGNAME</ref> is &quot;clinics&quot;, and every <ref refid="classCase" kindref="compound">Case</ref> permission query joins this table to recover the owning clinic&apos;s owner, admin and staff columns. The route layer selects <ref refid="classServiceController" kindref="compound">ServiceController&lt;Clinics&gt;</ref> / <ref refid="classStaffController" kindref="compound">StaffController&lt;Clinics&gt;</ref> for clinic endpoints. Without this file there would be no C++ type tying the <computeroutput>clinics</computeroutput> table to the CRUD machinery, and the permission system could not tell which clinic owns a given case.</para>
<para>CONCEPTS TO LEARN (for beginners):<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>static constexpr: a value fixed at compile time that belongs to the class, not to one object. <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/static">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/static</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>JSON: a text format used to carry request data; this project uses the jsoncons library to read it. <ulink url="https://jsoncons.github.io/jsoncons/">https://jsoncons.github.io/jsoncons/</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Template (<computeroutput>template &lt;typename T&gt;</computeroutput>): a blueprint that works with whatever data type the caller supplies. <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp</ulink></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><simplesect kind="author"><para>Amr Osman <ulink url="mailto:dr3mro@gmail.com">dr3mro@gmail.com</ulink> </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="date"><para>2026-11-16 </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
